1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,020
Watch this equation.

2
00:00:02,020 --> 00:00:04,280
The three walks across the equals sign.

3
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:06,920
Eleven minus three becomes eight.

4
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:09,020
Divide both sides by two.

5
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:10,340
And there's the answer.

6
00:00:10,340 --> 00:00:13,200
What makes this satisfying
is what doesn't disappear.

7
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:16,640
The two x and the equals sign
never vanish and reappear;

8
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:20,260
they just glide a little, so your
eye can follow only what changed.

9
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:23,580
By the end of this episode you will be
able to write that animation yourself,

10
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:25,880
and to understand why
letters and numbers are,

11
00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:28,500
to Manim, just more shapes.

12
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:31,980
Manim has three main ways
to put words on screen.

13
00:00:31,980 --> 00:00:35,320
Text uses ordinary fonts,
like a word processor.

14
00:00:35,320 --> 00:00:37,160
It is the right choice for titles,

15
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:38,900
labels and sentences.

16
00:00:38,900 --> 00:00:41,400
Font size controls how big it is.

17
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:43,320
Math Tex is for formulas.

18
00:00:43,320 --> 00:00:45,680
You write them in the notation of TeX,

19
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:49,300
the typesetting system that
mathematicians have used for decades:

20
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:52,600
a caret for superscripts,
backslash pi for pi,

21
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:55,100
backslash frac for fractions.

22
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:57,940
Math Tex treats the whole
string as mathematics.

23
00:00:57,940 --> 00:01:01,460
And Tex is for sentences
that mix words and maths.

24
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:05,460
Ordinary text, with the mathematical
parts wrapped in dollar signs.

25
00:01:05,460 --> 00:01:07,320
One habit to pick up straight away:

26
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:10,000
put a lowercase r in
front of these strings.

27
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,120
It makes it a raw string, which tells
Python to leave backslashes alone.

28
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:18,540
Without it, Python might read backslash
f in backslash frac as a special

29
00:01:18,540 --> 00:01:22,583
character, and the formula
breaks in confusing ways.

30
00:01:22,583 --> 00:01:25,583
How does a formula become
something Manim can animate?

31
00:01:25,583 --> 00:01:26,863
In standard Manim,

32
00:01:26,863 --> 00:01:30,683
your formula is handed to a real
installation of LaTeX on your computer.

33
00:01:30,683 --> 00:01:36,223
LaTeX typesets it, another tool converts
the result to scalable vector graphics,

34
00:01:36,223 --> 00:01:39,483
and Manim reads those
outlines in as cubic curves,

35
00:01:39,483 --> 00:01:41,763
exactly like the ones from last episode.

36
00:01:41,763 --> 00:01:46,203
That is why installing Manim
traditionally means installing LaTeX too,

37
00:01:46,203 --> 00:01:47,363
which is a large download.

38
00:01:47,363 --> 00:01:50,463
ManimOnline cannot assume you have LaTeX,

39
00:01:50,463 --> 00:01:51,463
so it takes a different route.

40
00:01:51,463 --> 00:01:56,343
It uses MathJax, a library that
typesets mathematics inside web pages,

41
00:01:56,343 --> 00:02:00,383
to turn your formula into glyph
outlines right in the browser.

42
00:02:00,383 --> 00:02:02,743
Python asks for the formulas it needs;

43
00:02:02,743 --> 00:02:05,783
the page draws them and
measures their exact sizes;

44
00:02:05,783 --> 00:02:08,643
then the scene renders a second
time using those measurements,

45
00:02:08,643 --> 00:02:12,363
so that formulas are placed with
their true widths and heights.

46
00:02:12,363 --> 00:02:13,343
The honest caveat:

47
00:02:13,343 --> 00:02:15,863
MathJax is not LaTeX.

48
00:02:15,863 --> 00:02:17,923
Everyday mathematical notation,

49
00:02:17,923 --> 00:02:20,583
fractions, roots, sums, integrals,

50
00:02:20,583 --> 00:02:23,723
matrices and Greek letters, works well.

51
00:02:23,723 --> 00:02:26,883
Custom LaTeX packages
and preambles do not.

52
00:02:26,883 --> 00:02:29,643
If a formula looks wrong in ManimOnline,

53
00:02:29,643 --> 00:02:30,963
simplify it first,

54
00:02:30,963 --> 00:02:36,333
and remember that full Manim with a real
LaTeX installation is the reference.

55
00:02:36,333 --> 00:02:38,693
Because text becomes outlines,

56
00:02:38,693 --> 00:02:41,213
every letter is a shape you can grab.

57
00:02:41,213 --> 00:02:42,973
Index a Text object like a list,

58
00:02:42,973 --> 00:02:43,953
and you get its letters.

59
00:02:43,953 --> 00:02:47,473
Zero is the first letter,
minus one the last.

60
00:02:47,473 --> 00:02:49,113
You can colour them, move them,

61
00:02:49,113 --> 00:02:52,913
and animate them one by
one with a lagged start.

62
00:02:52,913 --> 00:02:55,713
Formulas work the same
way, with one extra option.

63
00:02:55,713 --> 00:02:58,653
If you give Math Tex several
strings instead of one,

64
00:02:58,653 --> 00:03:00,493
each string becomes a separate part,

65
00:03:00,493 --> 00:03:03,533
and the parts are typeset
together as one formula.

66
00:03:03,533 --> 00:03:06,433
Index the formula, and you get the parts.

67
00:03:06,433 --> 00:03:09,653
Here a squared is part zero,
the plus sign is part one,

68
00:03:09,653 --> 00:03:13,353
and so on. That makes it
easy to colour a and b and c,

69
00:03:13,353 --> 00:03:16,583
or to point at one term
while you talk about it.

70
00:03:16,583 --> 00:03:19,583
Text has the styling options you'd expect.

71
00:03:19,583 --> 00:03:22,263
Weight makes it bold,
slant makes it italic,

72
00:03:22,263 --> 00:03:24,623
and font chooses a typeface.

73
00:03:24,623 --> 00:03:27,743
To colour particular words, pass t two c,

74
00:03:27,743 --> 00:03:29,423
short for text to colour:

75
00:03:29,423 --> 00:03:31,383
a dictionary from words to colours.

76
00:03:31,383 --> 00:03:33,823
For finer control there's Markup Text,

77
00:03:33,823 --> 00:03:36,183
which understands a small set of tags,

78
00:03:36,183 --> 00:03:37,743
a bit like a web page:

79
00:03:37,743 --> 00:03:41,943
b for bold, i for italic, and
span for colours and sizes.

80
00:03:41,943 --> 00:03:44,843
The tags shape the text,
and never appear in it.

81
00:03:44,843 --> 00:03:46,563
Formulas have their own trick.

82
00:03:46,563 --> 00:03:51,303
Tex to color map finds every occurrence
of a piece of TeX and colours it.

83
00:03:51,303 --> 00:03:54,463
Here every x becomes
blue and every y green,

84
00:03:54,463 --> 00:03:57,583
even the x and the y
tucked inside two x y.

85
00:03:57,583 --> 00:03:58,683
To make that possible,

86
00:03:58,683 --> 00:04:02,763
Manim isolates each of those symbols
as its own piece of the formula,

87
00:04:02,763 --> 00:04:05,543
so you can also animate them,
which comes in handy in a moment.

88
00:04:05,543 --> 00:04:09,063
Colour is one of the best
tools you have for explanation.

89
00:04:09,063 --> 00:04:12,823
If x is always blue, in every
formula and on every graph,

90
00:04:12,823 --> 00:04:15,950
the viewer stops having to read the label.

91
00:04:15,950 --> 00:04:17,690
Now the equation from the start.

92
00:04:17,690 --> 00:04:20,910
Each line of working is a
Math Tex with several parts.

93
00:04:20,910 --> 00:04:25,150
And the animation between lines
is called Transform matching tex.

94
00:04:25,150 --> 00:04:27,810
It looks at the parts of the
old formula and the new one,

95
00:04:27,810 --> 00:04:30,250
and pairs up any parts
whose TeX is identical.

96
00:04:30,250 --> 00:04:32,430
Two x goes with two x.

97
00:04:32,430 --> 00:04:34,830
The equals sign goes with the equals sign.

98
00:04:34,830 --> 00:04:36,430
The three goes with the three,

99
00:04:36,430 --> 00:04:38,310
even though it ends up somewhere else.

100
00:04:38,310 --> 00:04:40,170
Each matched pair is transformed,

101
00:04:40,170 --> 00:04:43,130
so it slides from its old
position to its new one.

102
00:04:43,130 --> 00:04:44,650
Parts with no match are handled gently:

103
00:04:44,650 --> 00:04:47,630
the old ones fade out,
the new ones fade in.

104
00:04:47,630 --> 00:04:50,070
In the second step, the
plus sign has no partner,

105
00:04:50,070 --> 00:04:53,550
and the minus sign is new, so
one fades as the other appears.

106
00:04:53,550 --> 00:04:55,730
The art is in how you split the strings.

107
00:04:55,730 --> 00:04:57,450
If you want two things to be matched,

108
00:04:57,450 --> 00:04:59,790
write them as separate, identical parts.

109
00:04:59,790 --> 00:05:01,930
If you write the whole
equation as one string,

110
00:05:01,930 --> 00:05:05,450
there's nothing to match, and the
entire formula simply cross-fades.

111
00:05:05,450 --> 00:05:09,517
So think of the parts as the pieces
you want the viewer's eye to follow.

112
00:05:09,517 --> 00:05:11,637
When working is written down a page,

113
00:05:11,637 --> 00:05:14,257
mathematicians line up the equals signs,

114
00:05:14,257 --> 00:05:16,537
so your eye can run
straight down the column.

115
00:05:16,537 --> 00:05:19,377
Manim won't do that automatically:

116
00:05:19,377 --> 00:05:23,817
arrange centres each line,
so the equals signs zig-zag.

117
00:05:23,817 --> 00:05:26,117
Because each equation is split into parts,

118
00:05:26,117 --> 00:05:28,057
though, the fix is short.

119
00:05:28,057 --> 00:05:31,437
The equals sign is part one of every line.

120
00:05:31,437 --> 00:05:33,897
Get x tells you how far across it is.

121
00:05:33,897 --> 00:05:39,077
So shift each later line sideways by the
difference between its equals sign and

122
00:05:39,077 --> 00:05:41,857
the first line's. Now
the column is straight,

123
00:05:41,857 --> 00:05:46,283
and anything that changes from line
to line stands out immediately.

124
00:05:46,283 --> 00:05:48,263
Explanations need pointing.

125
00:05:48,263 --> 00:05:52,403
A Brace is a curly bracket that
stretches to fit whatever you give it.

126
00:05:52,403 --> 00:05:54,823
Here, formula from part two onwards:

127
00:05:54,823 --> 00:05:56,963
everything after the equals sign.

128
00:05:56,963 --> 00:06:01,723
Ask the brace for get text, and it
places a label neatly beside itself.

129
00:06:01,723 --> 00:06:04,263
And Title gives you a heading
at the top of the frame,

130
00:06:04,263 --> 00:06:06,003
with a line beneath it.

131
00:06:06,003 --> 00:06:09,983
Small things, but they do a lot of the
work of making a scene feel like an

132
00:06:09,983 --> 00:06:13,300
explanation rather than a picture.

133
00:06:13,300 --> 00:06:16,420
Here is a small scene that
uses most of today's ideas.

134
00:06:16,420 --> 00:06:19,320
A right triangle, with
a square on each side.

135
00:06:19,320 --> 00:06:23,620
The square on the hypotenuse is positioned
using a little vector arithmetic from

136
00:06:23,620 --> 00:06:26,520
episode two: a vector as
long as the hypotenuse,

137
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:28,040
at right angles to it.

138
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:29,860
Each square gets a label, a squared,

139
00:06:29,860 --> 00:06:31,360
b squared, c squared.

140
00:06:31,360 --> 00:06:35,380
Then Transform from copy sends a copy
of each label flying to its place in the

141
00:06:35,380 --> 00:06:37,660
equation, while the originals stay put.

142
00:06:37,660 --> 00:06:39,860
The colours tie each
term back to its square,

143
00:06:39,860 --> 00:06:41,900
so the viewer can see what
the equation is saying,

144
00:06:41,900 --> 00:06:43,700
not just read it.

145
00:06:43,700 --> 00:06:46,620
Text is for words, Math Tex for formulas,

146
00:06:46,620 --> 00:06:48,680
and Tex for sentences that mix them.

147
00:06:48,680 --> 00:06:52,540
Every glyph becomes outlines, so letters
and terms are shapes you can index,

148
00:06:52,540 --> 00:06:55,300
colour and move. Split
formulas into parts,

149
00:06:55,300 --> 00:06:59,520
and Transform matching tex will
slide the pieces that stay the same.

150
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:04,180
So far, everything we have animated
changes only when we explicitly tell it to.

151
00:07:04,180 --> 00:07:07,480
Next time, we make objects that
keep themselves up to date:

152
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:09,600
labels that follow, numbers that count,

153
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:12,590
and shapes that redraw
themselves every single frame.
